EVA License and Public Work Notice

This notice is not a substitute for legal review. Questions about redistributing
EVA, or about combining it with licensed third-party code, should go to the
appropriate NIH technology transfer or software release office.


1. EVA's own code

Unless otherwise stated in a specific file or in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md, EVA was
developed by P. Molfese, National Institutes of Health (NIH), as part of official
duties for the United States Government.

This software is a "work of the United States Government" under 17 U.S.C. § 105
and is not subject to copyright protection within the United States.
International copyright protections may apply.

To the extent that any rights can be asserted by the United States Government,
the Government makes its own contributions to EVA available for public use,
copying, modification, and redistribution without fee or copyright restriction.

Nothing elsewhere in this notice narrows that grant. The Government-authored
files remain a public work in their own right: they may be extracted from EVA
and reused under no conditions at all, whatever license governs the assembled
application.


2. Third-party components

EVA incorporates, adapts, or redistributes third-party software, models, data,
and documentation that are subject to their own licenses and attribution
requirements. Those licenses govern those components.

The United States Government cannot and does not waive, grant, or modify rights
held by third parties. Where a third-party license imposes conditions —
attribution, notice retention, source availability, or copyleft — those
conditions apply in full to the covered material.

Per-file headers carry local attribution. THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md collects the
corresponding notices and identifies which EVA files are affected.


3. Copyleft components, and EVA as a whole

Some third-party components may be licensed under the GNU General Public License
or another copyleft license. Copyleft terms reach beyond the covered files: where
such a component is incorporated into a build, that license governs the covered
component and may govern the distribution of EVA as a whole, in both source and
binary form.

Where that is the case:

  - The covered files are marked with an SPDX-License-Identifier in their header
    and are listed in THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md. Files that do not carry such a
    marker are Government-authored and fall under section 1.

  - EVA as distributed is offered under the terms of the applicable copyleft
    license, and the corresponding source is made available as that license
    requires.

  - Section 1 still holds for the Government-authored files themselves. A public
    work imposes no conditions, so it combines with copyleft-licensed code
    without conflict; the conditions come from the third-party license, not from
    the Government's contribution.

At the time of writing, EVA incorporates no copyleft-licensed code. This section
states the terms that apply if and when it does, so that the intent is recorded
in advance rather than settled after the fact.


4. Warranty and liability

EVA is provided "as is", without warranty of any kind, express or implied,
including but not limited to warranties of merchantability, fitness for a
particular purpose, and noninfringement. In no event shall the United States
Government, NIH, or the contributors be liable for any claim, damages, or other
liability arising from use of EVA.
